ts on fundamentals and ratings"], "rationale": "The district's R+10 lean, Schmidt's incumbency and fundraising edge, and a unanimous Safe/Solid R consensus among nine raters all point firmly Republican, and Polymarket prices Democratic chances near 12 percent. The margin lands modestly beyond the fundamentals-only estimate to reflect that consensus.", "watch": "A late surge of Democratic spending or polling showing Coover within single digits would be the first sign the race is closer than raters expect.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derek Schmidt (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kansas' 2nd District is a first-term defense for Republican Derek Schmidt, who won his primary handily in August and holds a clear fundraising advantage over Democrat Don Coover, a physician who has raised about $759,000 but trails in cash on hand. A Libertarian candidate also appears on the ballot.\n\nNo public general-election polling is available, so the picture rests on the district's R+10 partisan lean, the incumbent's position, and expert ratings: all nine raters call the race Safe or Solid R, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-eight chance. Schmidt's bipartisan work on savings-account legislation has drawn some attention, but nothing suggests a competitive race. A Democratic win would require a substantial late shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0506, "margin": -10.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0308, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0524, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0445, "margin": -12.0}]}